BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Indigenous Voices: Greg Sarris & Jennifer Foerster in Conversation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T203000
DTSTAMP:20261108T061419Z
UID:dothebay-18029364@sf-cal
SEQUENCE:2
CATEGORIES:Literature
DESCRIPTION:Join us as we welcome Greg Sarris and Jennifer Foerster to sha
 re their latest works — The Last Human Bear and The Maybe-Bird. This eve
 ning kicks off our Native American Heritage Month series\, celebrating Nat
 ive voices in literature and community.\n\nFree to attend — RSVP require
 d to reserve your seat.\n\nSpeakers\nGreg Sarris is an enrolled member of 
 the Federated Indians of Graton Rancheria and is currently serving his sev
 enteenth consecutive elected term as chairman of the tribe. He is the auth
 or of several books\, including the novel Grand Avenue\, which he adapted 
 for an HBO miniseries and co-executive produced with Robert Redford\; the 
 novel Watermelon Nights\; Weaving the Dream\, a biography of Mabel McKay\;
  Becoming Story\, a memoir\; and the story collections How a Mountain Was 
 Made and The Forgetters. Formerly a full professor of English at UCLA\, Sa
 rris serves on the University of California Board of Regents and the Sunda
 nce Institute Board\, and he holds the Distinguished Emeritus Graton Endow
 ed Chair in Native American Studies at Sonoma State University. He lives i
 n Sonoma County\, California. (Photo by Beowulf Sheehan)\n\nJennifer Elise
  Foerster is the author of three books of poetry\, The Maybe Bird (The Son
 g Cave\, 2022)\, Bright Raft in the Afterweather (University of Arizona Pr
 ess\, 2018)\, and Leaving Tulsa (University of Arizona Press\, 2013)\, and
  served as the Associate Editor of When the Light of the World Was Subdued
 \, Our Songs Came Through: A Norton Anthology of Native Nations...\n\nFree
 \nTelegraph Hill Arts and Literature Presents:\n\nDetails: https://dotheba
 y.com/events/2026/11/7/indigenous-voices-greg-sarris-jennifer-foerster-in-
 conversation-tickets\nSource: DoTheBay
